# The canvas conformance scene (docs/canvas-plan.md): the core
# rasterizes, the backend blits, and three verbs say so.
#
# THE THREE ARE NOT REDUNDANT, and the pairing is the whole design (§7).
# `expect_drawing_hash` proves that this platform's libkaya produced the
# SAME PIXELS as every other platform's, down to antialiasing — the
# assertion React Native Skia built a whole screenshot suite to get,
# expressed as one frozen string, and the reason the buffer is core-owned
# at all. It proves NOTHING about whether anything reached the screen.
# `expect_drawing` is the legible one: how many ops the core replayed and
# where the ink landed, so a human reading a failure learns whether
# nothing arrived, whether the transform is wrong, whether the y axis is
# inverted, or whether the drawing sits in a corner of its track — none
# of which a hash can say. `expect_ink` is the only one that fails when
# the BLIT dropped: it samples the window's own rendered pixels, so a
# buffer that never reached the platform's image object, or reached it
# swizzled, upside down or at the wrong size, dies here while the hash
# stays green.
#
# THE HASH IS ONE STRING ON FIVE PLATFORMS because every input to it is
# one thing: the op stream (this file is shared verbatim and the guest
# draws in VIEWBOX units, so no platform's track can move a coordinate),
# the font bytes (kaya's own, embedded in libkaya), the shaper and the
# rasterizer (one pinned CPU-only crate set compiled into every lane's
# libkaya), the palette (kaya's own two-mode chart palette) and the scale
# (the verb pins it at 1.0, so the lane's display cannot reach it).
#
# ALL FIVE LANES ARE aarch64 TODAY (§7.1) — mac and iOS on Apple silicon,
# windows on aarch64-pc-windows-msvc, android on arm64-v8a, linux in a
# container on the same host — so the one thing that could falsify this
# string was cross-ISA identity of a SIMD raster pipeline, and it was
# MEASURED before the string was frozen: both architectures rasterize
# the same shapes to the same bytes
# (docs/measurements/canvas-cross-isa-2026-08-26.txt, which also carries
# the caveat that the x86_64 side was emulated). If a native x86_64 lane
# ever disagrees, this degrades to a hash per architecture family — still
# one string per lane family, and still stronger than anything a
# per-backend rasterizer could offer.
#
# No opening settle: the first expect's bounded retry IS the scene-ready
# wait.
expect label#0 "portfolio value"
# The legible pair first, so a failing run reads top to bottom as a
# diagnosis rather than as a hash mismatch with no context. 41 ops: the
# ground rect's six, three gridline pairs plus their stroke, the area's
# eleven, the series' eight, the axis' three, and two font selections
# with four text runs between them.
#
# THE FIGURE IS INSET INSIDE ITS VIEWBOX, so these four numbers can
# discriminate at all: a drawing that filled its box would read
# 0,0,100,100 whatever the transform did, and an inverted y axis or a
# drawing pushed into a corner would move nothing.
expect_drawing canvas@chart "41/2,8,97,83"
expect_drawing_hash canvas@chart "e5ac8a2c0b240633"
# THE BLIT, sampled at flat-region CENTRES and never at a boundary —
# expect_app_icon's measured discipline, because whatever rescale a
# platform applies blurs a boundary and would make this verb report a
# colour no palette entry has. 15,20 is plot ground above the series;
# 70,63 is deep inside the filled area under it.
#
# THE APPEARANCE IS PART OF THE OBSERVATION. The DISPLAY raster uses the
# platform's mode and kaya's palette has two of them (§6), so a bare
# colour string would be a frozen expectation quietly depending on the
# host's appearance setting. Naming the mode makes a dark-mode host fail
# with a sentence that says why. Making the ink assertion
# appearance-independent is phase 4's business, where the palette gets
# looked at hardest in both modes (docs/deferred.md).
#
# THESE ARE THE CORE'S OWN BYTES, and this verb alone compares them
# WITHIN ±1 PER CHANNEL (ruled 2026-08-26, §7.2). A macOS window's
# backing store carries the DISPLAY's profile, so the mac samples D2E2F7
# back off its own window where the core wrote D2E3F7 and Android's
# PixelCopy reports D2E3F7 — no one frozen string can exact-match both,
# and the byte-exact assertion is the hash above, which crosses no
# colour space. Freeze from the core (crates/kaya/src/canvas.rs's
# the_scene_probe_points_are_opaque_and_pinned prints both pixels), never
# from a platform's read.
expect_ink canvas@chart "15,20 70,63 = light FFFFFF/D2E3F7"
# AND THE CANVAS IS A WIDGET, with a widget's universal props: a drawing
# is an IMAGE to every platform's accessibility tree and the app authors
# the spoken name (§9). Nothing here builds a semantics tree out of the
# ops, deliberately — that is the one place an app opts out of kaya's
# accessibility dividend, and the honest contract is an image with a
# name.
expect_ax canvas@chart "image/Portfolio value"
